                <pb facs="0624" n="490" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg490"/>

                <head>No. 346.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Reinberg to Mr. Rives.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulate-General, of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Guayaquil</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1888-10-17"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >October</hi> 17, 1888</date>. (Received November 14.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 161.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to inform the Department that a
                    decree has been passed by the last Ecuadorian Congress, to take effect from
                    January 1, 1889, allowing vessels flying foreign flags to engage in the river
                    and coasting trade of Ecuador. This was formerly allowed only to vessels flying
                    the national flag, the Government reserving the right to stop all traffic and
                    close any or all ports during a time of revolution or in the event of a war with
                    any foreign nation.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Martin Reinberg</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Vice-Consul
                    General.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
